  "name": "Question 35",
  "problem": "Consider the vector space $C[-1,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-1,1]\\to R$ and its subset $A={f\\in C[-1,1]\\,|\\,f(1)\\le 0}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is closed under function addition\n- $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $A$ is a subspace of $C[-1,1]$\n- $A$ is **not** a subspace of $C[-1,1]$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(x),g(x)\\in A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(1) & =f(1)+g(1)\\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)\\in A$ and $k=-1\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & \\le 0 \\\\ kf(1) & \\ge k\\cdot 0 \\\\ (kf) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $A$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"",
